Parity mapping (web -> native):
  • c / f props                 -> celsius / fahrenheit inputs (renamed: SwiftLint identifier_name
                                   min_length:2 forbids single-char `c`/`f`; .swiftlint.yml is out of
                                   allowed-files so the idiomatic names are used — data/behaviour parity
                                   is preserved, only the Swift label differs).
  • useUnits().unitPrefs        -> \.tsUnits environment (P1/S8 seam) -> UnitPreferences bag.
  • convertTempFromSI(c, '°C')  -> identity;  convertTempFromSI(c, '°F') -> c*9/5+32 (affine, identical).
  • f-input normalize           -> (f - 32) * 5 / 9  (SI Celsius base).
  • fmtNumber(v, precision)     -> NumberFormatter (locale grouping, fixed digits, halfUp rounding);
                                   precision precedence prop -> pref -> 2, clamp 0...20 (setGlobalPrecision).
  • `{display}{tempUnit}`       -> NO separating space (e.g. "20°C") — verified against web Format.test.tsx
                                   ("renders 20°C as 20°C", "...as 68°F", "68°F -> 20°C", null -> "—").
  • title `${v.toFixed(1)} °C`  -> fixed 1-fraction-digit tooltip WITH a space (e.g. "20.0 °C"); surfaced
                                   via SwiftUI .help (web `title`).
  • null/non-finite -> "—"      -> empty branch (em-dash + localized VoiceOver label).

Parity note — states. The web source is a synchronous formatter (props + the useUnits() settings read;
NO fetch), so it has exactly two render branches: the formatted value and the "—" empty sentinel. It has
no loading/error/stale/offline axis. Synthesising network chrome would invent state the source does not
have. Both real branches are reproduced in full (value + empty). Same sanctioned disposition as 0085
Distance and 0075 AnimatedNumber.

